"Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure – Yoël Forterre (https://yoelforterre.wordpress.com/2026/06/18/fast-cell-wall-softening-causes-venus-flytrap-closure/)\nciteturn21view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://yoelforterre.wordpress.com/2026/06/18/fast-cell-wall-softening-causes-venus-flytrap-closure/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 99\nL0: cite0†Yoël Forterre L1: \nL2: Laboratoire IUSTI, CNRS Aix-Marseille Université\nL3: \nL4: [Button: Menu]\nL5: \nL6:   * cite0†Home L7:   * cite1†Research L8:   * cite2†Publications L9: # Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure\nL10: \nL11: Our paper on the Venus flytrap, with Jeongeun Ryu, Mathieu Colombani, Corentin Mollier, and Joël Marthelot, has just been published in Science and is featured on the cover!\nL12: \nL13: J. Ryu, M. Colombani, C. Mollier, J. Marthelot, Y. Forterre. cite3†Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure. Science, 2026, 392 (6803), pp.1183-1187†www.science.org L14: \nL15: cite4†Image L16: The carnivorous Venus flytrap is an iconic example of rapid plant movement and has fascinated scientists and the public alike since Darwin’s time. Although its ultrafast trap closure is known to involve an elastic instability, analogous to the snap-through buckling of a thin shell, the active mechanism triggering this motion has remained elusive.\nL17: In this study, we combine multiscale in situ mechanical and hydraulic measurements—from the whole organ down to the subcellular level—to show that trap closure is powered by a rapid and localized softening of the outer epidermal cell wall, which releases elastic energy stored in the tissue. This softening occurs within just a few seconds and results in a 40% decrease in wall stiffness, representing what is likely the fastest modulation of plant cell wall mechanics reported to date.\nL18: These findings challenge conventional models based on osmotic flow and hydraulic transport and provide a new framework for understanding rapid plant movements, with potential applications in bioinspired actuators, soft robotics, and smart materials.\nL19: \nL20: Link for downloading the published version of the paper: cite3†https://www.science.org/eprint/6MSZFZC9CCTACUE6SFFZ/full?activationRedirect=/doi/full/10.1126/science.aed5051†www.science.org L21: Accepted version on HAL, including the methods and the supplementary information: cite5†https://hal.science/view/index/docid/5658677†hal.science L22: \nL23: See also the Perspective “Exceeding nature’s biological speed limits” by Jacques Dumais in the same issue: cite6†https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aei3453†www.science.org L24: \nL25: cite7†VideoPress Video Player†video.wordpress.com L26: \nL27: Like\nL28: \nL29: cite8†Image: Unknown's avatar†1.gravatar.com cite9†yoelforterre cite10†June 18, 2026June 18, 2026 L30: ## One thought on “Fast cell wall softening causes Venus flytrap closure”\nL31: \nL32:   1. Pingback: cite11†Mucholapka nefunguje jako hydraulika. Nová studie odhalila, že past spouští sekundové změknutí buněčných stěn - VědaŽivě.cz†vedazive.cz L33: \nL34: Comment\nL35: \nL36: Name[Input]\nL37: \nL38: Email[Input]\nL39: \nL40: Website[Input]\nL41: \nL42: [Input]\nL43: \nL44: Turn on JavaScript to leave a comment.\nL45: \nL46: [Input] [Input]\nL47: \nL48: [Input]\nL49: \nL50: cite12†Previous Transient flows and migration in granular suspensions: key role of Reynolds-like dilatancy L51: ## CONTACT\nL52: \nL53: cite13†Image: DSC00411.JPG†yoelforterre.files.wordpress.com L54: \nL55: Yoël Forterre\nL56: Directeur de Recherche au CNRS\nL57: \nL58: cite14†Laboratoire IUSTI†iusti.cnrs.fr L59: cite15†SOFT team†iusti.cnrs.fr L60: UMR 7643 CNRS Aix-Marseille Université\nL61: 5 rue Enrico Fermi\nL62: 13453 Marseille Cedex 13\nL63: \nL64: yoel.forterre@univ-amu.fr\nL65: tel: 04 91 10 68 76\nL66: fax: 04 91 10 69 69\nL67: cite0†https://y